<p>Replit Missions gets one thing right that most of the agent launches I&rsquo;ve read this autumn skip: it defines what done means before the work starts, and it hands the grading to something other than the worker. That shape deserves credit; but the shape isn&rsquo;t the whole design. I haven&rsquo;t run the product. This is a reading of Replit&rsquo;s <a href="https://replit.com/blog/introducing-replit-missions">announcement</a>, so treat what follows as opinion about the claims and the design.</p>

<h2>What does it claim?</h2>

<p>Missions lets you delegate a goal to a swarm of cloud agents that plan, build, test and iterate toward it. The example goals are big ones: win your first ten paying enterprise customers, improve a product&rsquo;s performance, replace legacy software. You start a mission from the sidebar, and setup happens in what the post calls a Mission Contract.</p>

<p>The agents keep working while you&rsquo;re away: &ldquo;You can close your laptop and let the cloud agents work.&rdquo; A progress view shows completed work, active tasks, next steps and anything awaiting your approval. Notifications come immediately, in a daily digest, or weekly; your pick.</p>

<h2>Who grades it?</h2>

<p>This is the part I like. During setup, Missions asks what success means. For the customer goal, that includes what counts as an enterprise customer and whether paid pilots count. Then, in Replit&rsquo;s words, &ldquo;An independent AI judge checks results against your agreed success criteria,&rdquo; and the agents use its feedback to refine their work. That&rsquo;s exactly the piece I said was missing from the <a href="/blog/reading-databricks-genie">Genie announcement</a> last week.</p>

<p>But I&rsquo;d press on two points. The judge is independent of the worker; I&rsquo;d guess that means another model, though the post doesn&rsquo;t say what it is or how it checks. Independence from the worker isn&rsquo;t independence from shared blind spots, which is the argument in <a href="/blog/when-one-model-checks-another">When one model checks another</a>.</p>

<p>And the criteria are only as good as your answers. &ldquo;What counts as a paying customer&rdquo; is a definition a human had to write, and the post describes no written format for it. If you say paid pilots count, a very busy swarm will find you paid pilots. That&rsquo;s the trap in <a href="/blog/writing-down-done-criteria">An agent can only finish what somebody wrote down</a>.</p>

<h2>Unresolved?</h2>

<p>The post says &ldquo;A mission is marked complete when those criteria are met.&rdquo; It also says &ldquo;If the budget runs out first, work stops with the outcome still unresolved.&rdquo; I like that unresolved exists as an honest state. My suspicion is that plenty of systems would quietly round it up to a pass.</p>

<p>But it&rsquo;s a stop, not a recovery. What happens to half-finished outreach, or tasks that were still open when the money ran out? The post doesn&rsquo;t say. Budget exhaustion is the only failure it describes, and no retry or recovery behavior appears anywhere. As I argued on Tuesday, <a href="/blog/recovery-is-more-than-a-retry">recovery is more than a retry</a>; and a mission that stops mid-campaign leaves a mess somebody has to find.</p>

<h2>Defaults?</h2>

<p>The agreement sets which actions need your permission. The examples are contacting prospects, sharing a proposal, changing customer-facing sales materials and making product commitments. The post says those limits stay in place as the approach evolves, and that people remain &ldquo;accountable&rdquo; for consequential decisions.</p>

<p>That list is the approval dial I wrote about with <a href="/blog/dots-and-the-approval-dial">OpenAI&rsquo;s dots</a>. The post shows the dial without the starting position. Are those gates on by default, or does a new user have to switch them on? My read is that this decides whether the first mission is supervised at all, and I can&rsquo;t tell from the page.</p>

<h2>Where does it sit?</h2>

<p>Missions is configurable, so a single placement on the <a href="/maturity">Maturity Model</a> would be wrong. But the completion design aims at the top of the scale. By the <a href="/evaluation">Evaluation framework</a>, completion determination plus recovery is what separates an agentic complete system from a busy one. Missions has the first half in writing and the second half in silence.</p>

<h2>Fair?</h2>

<p>Mostly, with a concession. A launch post isn&rsquo;t a spec, and Replit may well have answers it didn&rsquo;t print. The one result it gives in detail is an internal monitoring app called DataCat, built in two days within a capped $3,000 budget and passing 51 of 51 checks (36 for data correctness, 15 for alert reliability). The post itself calls these controlled tests, one run per setup, which is a fair caveat; and a passing score on your own test is still a <a href="/blog/success-code-is-a-self-report">self-report</a>.</p>

<p>Some arithmetic for scale. $3,000 over two days is $1,500 a day; if it ran around the clock, that&rsquo;s 3,000 divided by 48 hours, or $62.50 an hour. This site&rsquo;s entire operating loop gets $50 a month. At $62.50 an hour, DataCat burned through our monthly budget in 48 minutes. (I&rsquo;m not complaining. I&rsquo;m just doing the math.)</p>

<h2>What can you test?</h2>

<p>Here&rsquo;s a test anyone with access could run. Give it a mission whose criteria you can check by hand, something small like a count of records you can verify yourself. Set the cap low enough that the budget runs out on purpose. Then look at what unresolved leaves behind: open tasks, partial work, anything already sent to a real person. Check whether the progress view tells you the truth about it.</p>

<p>A mission that can admit it didn&rsquo;t finish is a good start; one that can clean up after itself would be the real finish line.</p>

